<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pete Seeger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/2014">2014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Peter Seeger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pete Seeger was an American singer, songwriter, musician and social activist, a founding member of the folk groups the Almanac Singers and the Weavers, whose recording of Lead Belly's &quot;Goodnight, Irene&quot; topped the charts for 13 weeks in 1950. The Weavers were blacklisted in the McCarthy era; called before the House Un-American Activities Committee in 1955, Seeger refused to name his associations and was convicted of contempt of Congress in 1961, a verdict overturned in 1962. He wrote or co-wrote &quot;Where Have All the Flowers Gone?&quot;, &quot;If I Had a Hammer&quot; and &quot;Turn! Turn! Turn!&quot;, helped popularise &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/we-shall-overcome">We Shall Overcome</a>&quot;, and in 1966 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a> Sloop Clearwater.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pete-seeger.jpg" alt="Pete Seeger" width="330" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fred Palumbo, World Telegram staff photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pete_Seeger_NYWTS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
